course: english
lesson: 54
title: Putting it together: reading and writing like a writer
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Reading like a writer means noticing the tricks an author uses, not just what happens: how the first line hooks you, how tension builds, how a twist surprises you.
explain: Then you borrow the tricks, not the words: a question opening, "show, don't tell" description ("her hands shook" instead of "she was scared"), and dialogue with a new line for each new speaker.
explain: A strong story often has a hook at the start, tension in the middle and sometimes a twist at the end that changes how you see everything before it.
explain: Good writers change their style for the reader: the same news ("we won the match") is chatty for a friend and polite and clear for the head teacher.
explain: Picture a boy in Glasgow saying "Ah'm gonnae write a wee story aboot ma dug" ("gonnae" means going to, "dug" means dog): dialect is fine in talk and in characters' speech, he can use Standard English for the narrator of a formal piece, and then he edits with the checklist and shares it proudly.
fact: Reading like a writer means noticing the techniques authors use so that you can use them too.
fact: Borrowing an author's techniques is good practice; copying their actual words and calling them yours is not.
fact: A hook is an opening that grabs the reader, such as a question, a sudden action or a line of speech.
fact: Tension makes the reader worry about what will happen next; a twist is a surprise that changes the meaning of the story.
fact: "Show, don't tell" means showing a feeling through actions and details: "her hands shook" shows fear.
fact: In written dialogue, speech marks go around the words spoken, and each new speaker starts a new line.
fact: A writer's toolkit is a list of techniques (openings, description, dialogue) to check before and while writing.
fact: Editing with a checklist (spelling, punctuation, paragraphs, does it make sense?) improves a piece of writing.
fact: Writers change their register for their reader: chatty for a friend, formal for a head teacher.
fact: Dialect is fine in talk and in characters' speech; formal writing usually uses Standard English.
fact: Writers are not born, they are made: practice makes progress.
fact: Praise is most useful when it says what was good; then pick one thing to improve.
fact: Slang to know: "boss" (Liverpool) and "slaps" (Gen Z) both mean great, and "heaps" (Australia) means very.
exercise: What does "read like a writer" mean? | Notice the tricks the author uses | Read only famous books | Read as fast as you can to finish first | Copy the whole book out by hand, neatly
exercise: What is fine to borrow from a book you love? | A technique, like a question opening | Whole paragraphs, copied word for word | The author's own name, for your story | The full ending, without any changes
exercise: Which opening is a hook? | Nobody knew the box was ticking. | This story is about a box. | Once there was a box. It was brown. | The box was in the room, I think.
exercise: What is a twist in a story? | A surprise that changes what it means | A long description of the weather | The title written at the top of the page | A list of all the characters at the start
exercise: Which line shows fear without saying it? | Her hands shook as she opened the door. | She was very, very scared of the door. | She felt scared and frightened and afraid. | She was so scared, and that is a fact.
exercise: In written dialogue, what do you do when a new person speaks? | Start a new line | Use a semicolon instead | Write it all in capitals | Carry on the same line
exercise: Kai says, "Me writing's boss now, I use all them tools." What does "boss" mean? | Great | In charge of others | Bossy and rude | Boring
exercise: What is a writer's toolkit? | A list of techniques to use | A pencil case full of pens | A big dictionary to copy from | A set of rules about spelling only
exercise: Which question belongs on an editing checklist? | Does each paragraph make sense? | Is my handwriting the biggest in class? | Did I use up the most pages of anyone? | Did I finish first?
exercise: Ella says, "My story slaps." What should she do next? | Feel proud, then improve one thing | Never change a word | Throw it in the bin and start again | Tell everyone else that theirs is rubbish
exercise: A mum in Sydney says, "Heaps proud of your writing, love." What does "heaps" mean? | Very | Piles of | A little bit | Not at all
exercise: You must tell the head teacher "we won the match". Which fits? | I am pleased to report that we won the match. | OMG we smashed it, they got destroyed, lol. | Yo sir, we won, innit, it was well good. | Won. Match. Yeah. That's it really.
exercise: Now the same news for a friend. Which fits? | We won!! You should've seen it | I wish to inform you that we were victorious | Dear Sir, the match concluded in a victory | It is hereby reported that the match was won
exercise: A boy in Glasgow says, "Ah'm gonnae write a wee story aboot ma dug." What will he write about? | His dog | His dad | A small boat | A doughnut he ate
exercise: Can a character in a story speak in dialect? | Yes, it can bring a character to life | No, characters must speak formal English | No, dialect is always wrong | Yes, but only if the character is a villain
exercise: Year 6 shares their best pieces with the class. Why? | It builds confidence, and others learn | So the teacher can mark it all faster | Because sharing is secretly a test | To show everyone who is the best
test: Your mate says, "You're born a writer or you're not, end of." Put him right. | Writing improves with practice, like any skill | He is right: it is all fixed when you are born | Only grown-ups can ever get better at writing | Reading and writing have nothing to do with it
test: Reggie, Gen Alpha: "I'll copy the whole first chapter of a book, it's giving genius." What is the problem? | That's copying; borrow techniques, not words | Nothing, because copying is reading like a writer | Nothing, as long as he changes the title first | He should copy out two whole chapters instead
test: What would happen to a story with no tension at all? | Readers might lose interest | It would be more exciting to read | It would get a twist all by itself | It would turn into a poem instead
test: Which line is "telling", not "showing"? | Tom was angry. | Tom slammed the door. | Tom's face went bright red. | Tom clenched both of his fists.
test: A cartoon writes a conversation on one line with no speech marks. Fix it. | Speech marks, and a new line per speaker | More exclamation marks on every word | All of it written in capital letters | Nothing; it is fine just as it is
test: Nan in Cork: "Ah, that story's grand, a stór, but the ending's a bit weak." ("Grand" means fine; "a stór" is Irish for my darling.) What should you do? | Keep what works and improve the ending | Throw away the whole story and give up | Ignore what she said, every single word | Change everything, including the start
test: Which opening uses a question hook? | Have you ever heard a fridge whisper? | It was a normal day in a normal house. | The fridge in the kitchen was white. | My name is Jo and I am nine years old.
test: A story ends: "…and the 'monster' in the attic was Grandad's old radio." What kind of ending is this? | A twist | A hook | A simile | A cliché
test: Ollie, rough: "Edit? Nah mate, it's done, I ain't checking nowt." ("Nowt" means nothing.) Why is he wrong? | Editing catches mistakes and makes it better | Editing always makes a story much worse | Only teachers are ever allowed to edit | Checking is only needed in spelling tests
test: Which reply to a friend's story is the most useful? | Your opening grabbed me; the middle could be shorter. | It's good, I guess, it was all right, I suppose. | Mine's better, to be honest, but yours is fine. | Meh. It was a story. It had words in it, I think.
test: Which ONE sentence has a hook, shows a feeling and has correct dialogue? | "Run!" Maya shouted, her voice cracking as the floor shook. | Maya was scared and said run because the floor was shaking. | "run" maya shouted. she was very scared of the floor. | Maya shouted run, she felt scared, the floor shook a lot.
exam: Identify the practice of observing the techniques employed by an author with a view to employing them oneself. | Reading as a writer | Plagiarism | Proofreading | Paraphrasing
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises a "twist". | A surprise that alters the story's meaning | An opening designed to engage the reader | A list of characters set before the text | A description of the weather of the setting
exam: It is given that a pupil must report one event to a friend and to the head teacher. Determine the appropriate course. | Adjust the register to suit each reader | Employ identical wording for both | Write formally to the friend alone | Omit the report to the head teacher
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes "showing" rather than "telling". | Her hands trembled. | She was afraid. | She felt great fear. | She was scared stiff.
